<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brisbane</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brisbane is the capital of Queensland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, on the Brisbane River near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moreton-bay">Moreton Bay</a>. It began in 1825 as a penal settlement for convicts who had reoffended in New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>, moved from Redcliffe, and was opened to free settlers in 1842. It became the capital of the new colony of Queensland in 1859 and is now Australia's third-largest city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brisbane.jpg" alt="Brisbane" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Olszewski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brisbane_CBD_seen_from_Kangaroo_Point,_2024,_01_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Brisbane lies in the south-east corner of Queensland, centred on the Brisbane River, whose mouth is about 15 km from the central business district at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moreton-bay">Moreton Bay</a>. The city spreads across the hilly floodplain of the river valley between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> and the Taylor and D'Aguilar ranges. It takes its name from the river, which was named for Sir Thomas Brisbane, governor of New South Wales from 1821 to 1825. The climate is humid subtropical, with hot, wet summers and mild, drier winters. Most central streets are named after members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a>, and Queen Street, named for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, is the traditional main street.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Brisbane region has been inhabited for more than 22,000 years by the Yagara (Yuggera), Turrbal and Quandamooka peoples. The river, known as Maiwar, formed the heart of cultural, economic and ceremonial life, and the central city peninsula was traditionally known as Meeanjin. Matthew Flinders charted parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moreton-bay">Moreton Bay</a> in 1799, and John Oxley found the river in 1823 with the help of castaways and recommended the area for a penal settlement. The penal station, first set up at Redcliffe, moved to North Quay in May 1825. Under Captain Patrick Logan it gained a reputation as one of the harshest in New South Wales, and it saw recurrent conflict with neighbouring Indigenous peoples. It was closed in 1842, opening the district to free colonisation.</p><p>German Lutherans founded the first free settlement, Zion Hill at Nundah, in 1838, and in 1859 Brisbane was chosen as capital when Queensland separated from New South Wales. In the early 20th century it saw labour unrest, including the 1912 Brisbane general strike. The creation of the City of Brisbane in 1925, by merging twenty smaller local government areas, formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s largest municipal authority. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Allied command in the South West Pacific was based in the city, together with the headquarters of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Brisbane is the capital of Queensland and the third most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, with about 2.8 million people in the metropolitan area. Its surviving early buildings include the Commissariat Store on William Street, built by convict labour in 1828, and the Old Windmill in Wickham Park, the oldest surviving building in the city. City Hall opened in 1930 and the Story Bridge in 1940. The city hosted the 1982 Commonwealth Games and World Expo 88, and has been chosen to host the 2032 Summer Olympics. Its surrounding region includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moreton-bay">Moreton Bay</a> and the Gondwana Rainforests, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brisbane” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For Expo 88 in <b>Brisbane</b>, Alan Slater made an up-tempo arrangement in both languages, sung by Annie Crummer, Peter Morgan, Dalvanius Prime and the Pātea Māori Club; TVNZ opened its broadcasts with it from 1988 to 1995.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a></li><li><blockquote>A penal settlement for reoffending convicts was set up at Moreton Bay in 1824 and moved to the site of <b>Brisbane</b> in 1825.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-queensland">Colony of Queensland</a></li></ul></section>
